  and spider-man sort of comes from the  same place in terms of that but I want  to say is more about momentum so when  you're swinging through the city which  is how the demo I played started out it  is so satisfying I was playing with a  friend who's sitting next to me at the  time and they're just moments where both  of us would just sort of be like oh my  god  basically as you start swinging it very  naturally finds points in the world for  you to rip your web onto and you really  feel that idea of a kind of pendulum  wait.you while building speed as you dip  down and then flip up and you keep  rising and obviously it's unrealistic  the amount of momentum you can build up  from one swing is crazy but because of  that it just feels really fast and  swinging I want to say is is very easy  it seems like you can latch on to  anything and everything  there were times where I plummeted  towards the ground but for the most part  it's very easy to see something to swing  and then run across the side of a  building and then leap off of that into  the end as this move where you can like  pull yourself towards a perch and you  can like sort of just shoot two webs at  it and then pull yourself forward and  then just keep going with that momentum  whereas obviously the akhom games kind  of do the opposite of that when it comes  to gliding I still had a lot of fun  gliding but the momentum slows down as  you glide rather than it being this this  build up I mean even when you're going  towards the ground in spider-man it's  like Criterion levels of like showing  you the speed where you're plummeting  towards the ground in the world is just  moving up around you I can't even  explain how good it feels but it does  feel like every new swing that you take  is building towards that sense of  momentum and you know the Aachen games  even when Knight was quite open there  are always areas that you couldn't go to  because there was snipers on the  rooftops of people on the streets where  is in spite a man being that he's your  friendly neighborhood spider-man it  feels like the world isn't particularly  hostile like you are free to swing  around that city it is your city and  maybe as the campaign goes on nobody  moments for those areas that you can't  go to without being attacked but it  definitely felt just very much all in  with you having fun with that system so  the way it comes to combat I expected  this to feel pretty similar to the  Arkham games and there are things in  common it has a vaguely familiar  countess system spidey-sense like  tingles above your head and you know  that someone who's going to shoot at you  or what have you  dodging is very quick and it does have  that sense of momentum with combat as  well where you punch someone it seems  like every punch you're kind of moving  further and further and and it's just  building up but the biggest difference  with spider-man one thing that I really  like about it is that a lot of your  punches seem to go toward  verticality basically a lot of your  moves are designed with the ability to  uppercut someone or web shoot someone  and have them up in the air when you can  really bring them up and you can shoot a  web with them and throw them back at the  ground or like pull someone and knock  them into someone else  it's just a lot of sort of instead of  you necessarily gliding around like  Batman does which you still can do a  Spidey he's very very agile it feels a  little more like you can move the other  enemies around environments a whole lot  including like pinning one guy up  against a wall waiting you know sort of  sealing him there with your web and then  going and attacking someone else who's  maybe on like a second story and just  like jumping all over this environment  like this kind of an athletic acrobatic  Spider Man would so because of that I  imagine environments are a lot more open  um more often and probably a lot taller  or more vertical more often and you know  one 